But this is a canonical combiner example! These are two anti-patterns in one example: don't use combiners in a reduce-heavy job and don't use R vectorized library functions when possible. Look just at the second one since I can cook up an example real quick

> system.time(sum(z))
   user  system elapsed
  0.002   0.000   0.001
> system.time({X = 0; for (x in z) X = X + x})
   user  system elapsed
  0.517   0.004   0.524

500 X slower!! Are you willing to wait 500 times longer or get hold of a cluster that's 500 times large (assuming linear scalability and assuming that it's feasible)?

As far as getting warmed up to the combiner approach, people sometimes seem to think that combiners are only good for sums. This paper <http://www.cs.utah.edu/~jeffp/papers/merge-summ.pdf> shows what can be done with several type of statistical summaries that can be assembled from summaries of subsamples with operations that are commutative and associative, that is compatible with combiners. Quantiles, heavy hitters, bloom filters, you name it. Highly recommended.

Antonio

